I wrote 59 hidden tests in 4 files under `packages/core/test/hidden-handles-check/`. The type-check (`npx tsc -p tsconfig.test.json`) reports no errors. Run against the current stubs, all 59 fail with `not implemented`.

To make sure the tests expect the right values, I ran them against a reference implementation I wrote in the scratchpad, not in the repo. It passed all 138 tests, public and hidden. I also tried four shortcut implementations that pass the public suite. Each was caught, and two were caught only by the hidden tests:
- **Cutting off the domain with `/@.*/`**: the dot doesn't match a newline, so `"dana@example\ntest"` gives the wrong stem.
- **Checking the length before lowercasing**: `"İ"` becomes two characters when lowercased, so `"İ".repeat(20)` or `"x".repeat(29) + "İ"` gets the wrong problem.

**Files:**
- **`check-handle.test.ts`**: the length constants and every length from 0 to 45, plus every disallowed ASCII character in every position. It also checks:
  - newlines in the middle, which catches a pattern with the multiline flag;
  - Unicode lookalikes, the Kelvin sign and the long s, which catch a case-insensitive Unicode pattern;
  - padding that only fits once trimmed;
  - `__proto__` and `constructor`, which catch a reserved-name lookup on a plain object;
  - emoji-only handles and combining marks at both length bounds.
- **`normalize-handle.test.ts`**:
  - every whitespace character `trim` removes, and the zero-width space it keeps;
  - no Unicode normalization, and plain `I` lowercased without the Turkish rule;
  - `display` after Unicode padding;
  - the Kelvin sign anywhere and at 30 characters;
  - `isValidHandle` agreeing with `normalizeHandle`.
- **`handle-stem.test.ts`**:
  - runs of emoji and Unicode collapsed to one hyphen;
  - `-_-` giving `_-user`, and `İ` giving `i-user` but `İİ` giving `i-i`;
  - long hyphen runs before letters, which catch a slow trim;
  - cuts that land on a hyphen or an underscore;
  - a check that stems of about 7,400 short awkward strings all pass `checkHandle`;
  - the email cases: first `@`, newlines after it, a blank local part.
- **`module.test.ts`**:
  - exact results for lone surrogates and control characters;
  - several valid handles in a row, which catches a shared pattern with the `g` flag;
  - the index functions called directly, and the existing title rules still working.

59 tests in 4 files.

Open in the spec: none
